## Proof

Let $H$ be a separable Hilbert space with orthonormal basis $(e_i)_{i\ge 1}$. Recall:

- **WOT** is generated by seminorms $p_{u,v}(T)=|(Tu,v)|$ for all $u,v\in H$.
- $\mathcal{T}$ is generated by seminorms $p_{i,j}(T)=|(Te_i,e_j)|$ for $i,j\ge 1$.

Since every $p_{i,j}$ is a special case of $p_{u,v}$ (with $u=e_i$, $v=e_j$), every $\mathcal{T}$-open set is WOT-open. Hence

$$\mathcal{T}\subseteq \text{WOT on all of } B(H).$$

We prove the reverse inclusion **on norm-bounded subsets**. Fix $M>0$ and let $\mathcal{B}_M=\{T\in B(H):\|T\|\le M\}$.

**Claim.** For every $u,v\in H$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, there exist finitely many index pairs $(i_1,j_1),\dots,(i_N,j_N)$ and $\delta>0$ such that for all $T\in\mathcal{B}_M$,
$$|(Te_{i_k},e_{j_k})|<\delta\ \text{for all }k\ \Longrightarrow\ |(Tu,v)|<\varepsilon.$$

Once the claim is proved, every basic WOT-neighborhood of $0$ (and by translation, of any $T_0$) intersected with $\mathcal{B}_M$ contains a basic $\mathcal{T}$-neighborhood intersected with $\mathcal{B}_M$. Hence WOT $\subseteq\,\mathcal{T}$ on $\mathcal{B}_M$, and together with the trivial inclusion we get equality on every norm-bounded set.

*Proof of the claim.* Write $u=\sum_i u_i e_i$ and $v=\sum_j v_j e_j$ with $\sum|u_i|^2=\|u\|^2<\infty$ and $\sum|v_j|^2=\|v\|^2<\infty$. Choose $\eta>0$ (to be fixed later) and pick $I,J\ge 1$ so large that
$$\Bigl(\sum_{i>I}|u_i|^2\Bigr)^{1/2}<\eta,\qquad \Bigl(\sum_{j>J}|v_j|^2\Bigr)^{1/2}<\eta.$$

Decompose $u=u_{\mathrm{h}}+u_{\mathrm{t}}$ where $u_{\mathrm{h}}=\sum_{i\le I}u_i e_i$ and $u_{\mathrm{t}}=u-u_{\mathrm{h}}$, and similarly $v=v_{\mathrm{h}}+v_{\mathrm{t}}$. Then $\|u_{\mathrm{t}}\|<\eta$, $\|v_{\mathrm{t}}\|<\eta$, and
$$(Tu,v)=(Tu_{\mathrm{h}},v_{\mathrm{h}})+(Tu_{\mathrm{h}},v_{\mathrm{t}})+(Tu_{\mathrm{t}},v).$$

For the two tail terms, using $\|T\|\le M$ and Cauchy–Schwarz:
$$|(Tu_{\mathrm{h}},v_{\mathrm{t}})|\le \|T\|\,\|u_{\mathrm{h}}\|\,\|v_{\mathrm{t}}\|\le M\|u\|\,\eta,$$
$$|(Tu_{\mathrm{t}},v)|\le \|T\|\,\|u_{\mathrm{t}}\|\,\|v\|\le M\,\eta\,\|v\|.$$

Choose $\eta>0$ so that $M\eta(\|u\|+\|v\|)<\varepsilon/2$. Then the total tail contribution is $<\varepsilon/2$ for every $T\in\mathcal{B}_M$.

The head is a finite linear combination:
$$(Tu_{\mathrm{h}},v_{\mathrm{h}})=\sum_{i\le I,\,j\le J}u_i\,\overline{v_j}\,(Te_i,e_j).$$

Set $C=\sum_{i\le I,j\le J}|u_i|\,|v_j|$ (a finite constant) and choose
$$\delta=\frac{\varepsilon}{2C}\quad(\text{with } \delta=1 \text{ if } C=0).$$

If $|(Te_i,e_j)|<\delta$ for all $i\le I$, $j\le J$, then
$$|(Tu_{\mathrm{h}},v_{\mathrm{h}})|\le\sum_{i\le I,j\le J}|u_i|\,|v_j|\,|(Te_i,e_j)|<C\cdot\delta=\varepsilon/2.$$

Combining head and tail,
$$|(Tu,v)|<\varepsilon/2+\varepsilon/2=\varepsilon.$$

The finitely many pairs $(i,j)$ with $i\le I$, $j\le J$ and the $\delta$ above witness the claim. $\square$

**Conclusion.** On $\mathcal{B}_M$, every WOT-neighborhood of any operator $T_0$ contains a $\mathcal{T}$-neighborhood of $T_0$ (apply the claim to $T-T_0$, noting $\mathcal{B}_M$ is not translation-invariant but the argument is applied to the bounded set $\mathcal{B}_M-T_0\subseteq\mathcal{B}_{M+\|T_0\|}$, or equivalently re-run the estimate with $T-T_0$ and the bound $\|T-T_0\|\le M+\|T_0\|$). Hence WOT $\subseteq\,\mathcal{T}$ on $\mathcal{B}_M$. Together with $\mathcal{T}\subseteq$ WOT, the two topologies coincide on every norm-bounded subset of $B(H)$.

**Yes, $\mathcal{T}$ and WOT agree on norm-bounded subsets of $B(H)$.**

